Twist TODO List
================

  [ ] RTSP
  [ ] SCP/SFTP, one handler over foxtrot (../foxtrot).  scp:// runs over
      SFTP; there is no SCP wire protocol.  Done: logging in with a key
      file or a password, known_hosts (~/.ssh/known_hosts,
      -k skips, no accept-new), a pinned fingerprint, downloading and
      uploading a file to each URL, resuming either way (-C), ranges (-r),
      directory listings, keys in an ssh-agent, keyboard-interactive login
      with the password, and one connection for the URLs of a command
      line.
      Still to do:
        - a transfer that fails, even for a missing file, closes its
          connection, so the next URL logs in again.  An SFTP status
          that leaves the channel sound could keep it.
        - a parked connection that the server closed an instant before
          it is taken fails the transfer with "closed" and does not retry
          on a new one.  The command line runs the loop
          once before each URL, which catches closes that have already
          arrived; the window left is a race.
        - nothing parked is sent a disconnect when it is dropped (idle
          for two minutes, or at exit); the TCP connection just closes.
        - no way to forbid reuse, in the library or in the tool.
        - ask foxtrot for the number of requests outstanding in an SFTP
          client.  gf_sftp_client_pending is the bytes queued, so twist
          counts its own (sftp.c sent () and inflight).
        - proxies: socks5:// is treated as socks5h:// (the name goes to the
          proxy to look up; socks5:// should look it up itself).
          socks4:// proxies are refused.  Credentials are
          sent for tunnels only: a plain http:// URL through an HTTP
          proxy gets no Proxy-Authorization.
        - statvfs, and the other commands the sftp tool has, as -Q
          commands; --create-file-mode and the directory mode.
        - quote commands for scp URLs.
        - an HTTP/3 URL does not take a zone: quic_connect reads the host
          with inet_pton itself.  Only the TCP dialers pass the scope.
        - --limit-rate for the other protocols: the pacer is in
          src/engine/rate.c; only the SFTP pumps use it.  Until then the
          command line refuses the option for other URLs.
        - certificates are found only as KEY-cert.pub beside a key file;
          there is no option to name a certificate elsewhere (ssh's
          CertificateFile).
        - -l for ftp URLs, which would send NLST; it only affects sftp
          now
        - -C and -T completion of the URL for the other schemes (done for
          sftp and scp only)
        - FIDO keys held in a file are not supported, and will not be: no
          libfido2.  Keys held by an ssh-agent work, with the agent doing
          the token I/O.
        - keyboard-interactive questions that need a person, such as a
          one-time code: twist answers only a single hidden question
          with the password.  A prompt on the terminal
          would need a way for the application to be asked.
      Requires libgcrypt >= 1.12.4.
  [ ] ftps (src/ftp/ftp.c).  The control connection is TLS from the first byte
      and the data connection is protected (PBSZ 0, PROT P) and resumes the
      control session.  Left:
        - session reuse needs a TLS 1.3 ticket from the control connection.
          Against a TLS 1.2 server, or one that gave none, the data
          connection does a full handshake, and a server that requires
          reuse then refuses it.
        - a data connection that ends without a close_notify is taken as the
          end of the file, as it is for ftp, so a truncated transfer is not
          noticed (the size in the 150 reply is parsed and not used for this).
        - no CCC, and no PROT C or PROT S choice: PROT P or nothing.
        - through an http:// or socks5:// proxy the data connection is
          tunnelled as well as the control connection (integration/
          ftp-proxy.sh, with plain ftp).  ftps through a proxy, which runs
          TLS on that tunnel, has not been run.
  [ ] Cookies (src/http/cookie.c).  No Public Suffix List: a Domain that is a
      top-level domain, or a country code with a generic second level (co.uk,
      com.au), is refused, and anything else is believed (github.io,
      blogspot.com).  Using libpsl is a dependency decision.  Expires and
      Max-Age are not read: a cookie lives as long as the jar, and a
      Max-Age=0 does not delete.  SameSite and HttpOnly are not looked at.
  [ ] Redirects.  A redirect is followed only to http, https, ws and wss; there
      is no --proto-redir to change that.
  [ ] STARTTLS for ldap, and a TLS upgrade for mqtt, are not done; curl has
      the first through OpenLDAP only, and mqtt has no upgrade command.  A
      user name and password for those schemes without the s go in the clear
      (the manual says so).  PLAIN and LOGIN are tried when an smtp, imap or
      pop3 server advertises no mechanism, and --ssl-reqd keeps them off the
      wire until the connection is encrypted.
  [ ] The output buffer of a connection has no limit.  A handler that queues
      without end against a peer that does not read grows it, so each handler
      limits itself (SFTP does, by its window); none does for a body given in
      one piece, which the application chose the size of.
  [ ] HTTP/3 closes the connection when the server opens a bidirectional stream
      (twist_quic_stream_action, tested), but the callback that does it
      (ev_stream_data in quic.c) is not run by any test: it needs a QUIC peer
      that opens one, and there is none in the tree.
  [ ] Discovered resolvers (RFC 9462).  A DoT or DoQ resolver found with a
      _dns.resolver.arpa query must have a certificate valid for the address
      that was asked as well as for its own name (twist_verify_chain_names,
      tested with a certificate in tests/test-tls.c).  The wiring from the
      resolver through twist_connection_require_name and the quic option has
      no test that runs it: the nameserver is read from /etc/resolv.conf, so a
      fake one cannot be put in front of twist without root.  The lookup of a
      named resolver's own host name still uses plain UDP, as it must.
  [ ] The HTTP pool's key is the loop, scheme, host, port and proxy; it has
      nothing of the TLS settings (verification, CA, client certificate,
      pinning).  A runtime whose transfers differ in them can
      use a connection made under others.  The SSH key hashes its
      equivalents (see ssh_pool_key in src/ssh/ssh.c).  The command line is
      unaffected because one operation has one set of options.
  [ ] --connect-timeout covers the greeting and login for SSH only.  The
      other protocols on the shared dialer (SMTP, POP3, IMAP, LDAP, ...) have
      it for the lookup and the TCP connect, and a server that takes the
      connection and sends no greeting is waited for until --max-time.  They
      can call twist_mail_link_phase_begin and _end when they are up.  FTP
      and HTTP dial themselves and do the same.
  [ ] Passphrase-protected OpenSSH-format private keys.  Blocked on
      bcrypt-pbkdf being available in libgcrypt; it is deliberately not
      taken from anywhere else.  An ssh-agent holding the key is the
      workaround, and works.
  [ ] Fuzzing covers the parsers in the library (see HACKING).  Not reached:
      the command line's own parsing (main.c: options, the speed and time
      arguments, --write-out), which is static there; the DNS cache and
      resolver state machines; the SFTP engine's use of foxtrot, which could
      be driven by a fuzzed server session; the Set-Cookie/HSTS persistence;
      and the file scheme.  A fuzzer of a whole transfer, with a scripted peer
      on a socketpair, would reach the connection and handler glue.
  [ ] The progress meter (see HACKING).  Left out, or different:
        - an upload is counted when the body is handed to the connection (HTTP,
          SMTP, MQTT, TFTP), not when the socket has taken it; sftp counts when
          the server has acknowledged a write.  A large POST on a slow link is
          at 100% sooner than it should be.
        - no size is known for POP3, LDAP, DICT, Gopher, WebSocket, MQTT or a
          directory listing.  For FTP the size is the number the reply to
          RETR may carry; asking the server (SIZE) would give it more often.
        - an HTTP response that is coded (--compressed) is counted by its
          bytes on the wire, which is what Content-Length counts;
          TWISTINFO_SIZE_DOWNLOAD is the decoded.
        - sftp and scp ask for the size of the file (one more round trip) only
          when the meter is on.
        - the tool's -s, -S, -# and --progress-meter have their --no- forms;
          the other boolean options do not yet.
        - --stderr - keeps standard error; it should write to standard output.
        - no warning that binary output is going to a terminal.
  [ ] The environment (see the manual).  Not done: a configuration file.
      A script that sets CURL_CA_BUNDLE gets the system's authorities;
      whether to read it is undecided.  SSL_CERT_DIR
      is read as a directory of PEM files by name (.pem, .crt, .cer or a
      c_rehash name), not through OpenSSL's rules.
  [ ] https proxies (engine/tunnel.c) are done for TCP: TLS to the proxy, then
      CONNECT in HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1.  Left:
        - CONNECT-UDP (RFC 9298, engine/masque.c) has no end-to-end test: the
          only proxy to test against was a Go program, which has been
          removed.  The unit tests (tests/test-masque.c) remain.  HTTP/3
          directly has none either.
        - CONNECT-UDP (RFC 9298, engine/masque.c) is done for HTTP/3, with
          --proxy-template-udp.  Left: HTTP/3 under a proxy is chosen only by
          --http3-only (the target's HTTPS record and Alt-Svc are not asked
          for, since the proxy does the lookup); DNS over QUIC does not use a
          proxy; the tunnel's packets are at most 1200 bytes (twist asks for
          that with max_udp_payload_size, or a peer's path MTU probes could
          exceed what the proxy's own datagrams hold, and some proxies then
          end the flow), and so a larger path MTU is not used.
        - templated CONNECT-TCP (draft-ietf-httpbis-connect-tcp), and
          --proxy-template-tcp for it, when it is an RFC.
        - ftp and ftps through an https proxy (the data connection too).
        - an http:// URL is tunnelled (CONNECT) and not sent in the proxy's
          absolute form, which some proxies would rather have.
        - a tunnel has an HTTP/2 connection of its own; one connection could
          carry many.
        - quickstep's gq_tls_free does not free the selected transcript
          (t->tr), so each TLS connection leaks about 1.3 KB.
  [ ] -D and -i show an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 response as its pseudo-header
      ":status: 200" and not as a status line "HTTP/2 200", and -w
      %{http_version} is empty.
  [ ] URL globbing is done for URLs and for -o names (#N).  Not done: globbing
      the file given to -T ({a,b} there, with #N in the URL), and #N in
      --output-dir.  -T takes the name as written.
  [ ] SMB/SMBS
  [ ] RTMP/RTMPS
  [ ] NTLM and Negotiate, only after an explicit dependency decision
  [ ] Decide how integration peers are provided.  make check stays
      in-process.  make check-integration dials a peer and skips with
      exit 77 when the peer is absent.  A podman lab is one candidate
      and is not chosen yet.
  [ ] io_uring poll backend
  [ ] The storage pool has two threads, shared by the file handler's reads
      and the SSH key and known_hosts loads.  Letting go of a file transfer
      no longer waits for its worker, but a read that never comes back
      still holds one of the two until the process ends, and two such
      reads hold up every file transfer and SSH load behind them.  A
      larger pool only moves the limit; a read that can be given up on
      would need a thread to be cancelled, or a different way to read.
